      "id": "delimiter-eof-enables-unread",
      "text": "Start with a buffer containing only 'x'. ReadByte returns 'x' and leaves Len() at zero. Call ReadBytes or ReadString with any delimiter. It returns empty data and io.EOF, then UnreadByte succeeds and the next ReadByte returns 'x' again. On a fresh zero-value buffer, the same delimiter read and UnreadByte also report EOF and success respectively, but there is no byte to restore. As a control, after draining 'x', a second ReadByte returns io.EOF and resets the buffer, so UnreadByte fails. A delimiter read that finds its delimiter returns data through that byte with nil error, and UnreadByte restores the delimiter.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 466,
        "fragment": "b.lastRead = opRead"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should delimiter reads that return io.EOF enable UnreadByte, including when they return zero bytes, given the documented error and zero-byte exclusions?"
    },
