      "id": "copy-and-storage-aliasing",
      "text": "Copy a zero-value Buffer before either value is used, then write a different byte to each. Each value gets its own backing storage and reads back its own byte. For a non-zero control, give NewBuffer a slice containing 'a' with capacity 4, then copy the Buffer value. WriteByte('b') through the original. The original now reads 'ab' and the copy still has length 1. WriteByte('c') through the copy next. Both buffers now expose 'ac' because both writes used the same backing-array slot. On a single buffer, mutating an element of Bytes() before the next buffer operation likewise changes what the next read returns.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 25,
        "fragment": "Copying and modifying a non-zero Buffer is prone to error,"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "readfrom-count-before-error",
      "text": "Start with unread data and call ReadFrom. Each iteration reserves at least MinRead bytes of tail space, then calls the supplied reader with the available tail. If the reader supplies bytes together with io.EOF, ReadFrom keeps the bytes, includes them in the returned count, and returns nil error. With another non-nil error, it still commits the bytes and returns the same error value. A (0, nil) result causes another iteration, so a reader that repeats it indefinitely keeps the call running. A negative count panics before that iteration extends the buffer or updates the count. Bytes committed on earlier iterations remain unread.",
